{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"6e45e89c4b2d0e14fc1a257484ab63d27e9f370978ea9efba66bef7a83e18350","slug":"blog2-model","location":"/blog2_model.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"What Drives the Life Expectancy Gender Gap?","content_includes_title":false,"github":"https://github.com/AllenDowney/EqualityWEF","source_url":"https://github.com/AllenDowney/EqualityWEF/blob/main/jb/blog2_model.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/AllenDowney/EqualityWEF/edit/main/jb/blog2_model.md","thumbnail":"/LifeExpectancy//build/blog2_coefficients_l-e58b08abbcb363959aa714a8d501ecb4.png","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"blog2_model.md","url":"/LifeExpectancy//build/blog2_model-53258f46e6e18f75c6de70a4dedfa8c5.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is Part 2 in a series on gender gaps in life expectancy, what causes them, and what we can do about it.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"lS3YuCT3SQ"}],"key":"b3C5Cvmqt4"}],"key":"HcUn7s1Tm1"},{"type":"thematicBreak","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"rWxlIeU26l"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"mhkGchnc6T"},{"type":"link","url":"https://allendowney.substack.com/p/the-gender-gap-in-life-expectancy","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"previous article","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"K8CSosL6eT"}],"urlSource":"https://allendowney.substack.com/p/the-gender-gap-in-life-expectancy","key":"zVgnThaZgu"},{"type":"text","value":", we showed that the gender gap in life expectancy varies between countries, from 2.5 years in Israel to 12.7 years in Lithuania, and in some countries it has changed substantially over time.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"YBMDKws16g"}],"key":"aLmz9ieaHf"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As a first step toward understanding these differences, we looked at death rates from several causes with large gender gaps: drug disorders, homicide, suicide, and road traffic.\nWe concluded that gender gaps in these death rates are contingent — that is, they are driven by history, economics, culture, and public policy.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"X4lQTFNCqE"}],"key":"fFtKZ1Krng"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now let’s see how changes in cause-specific death rates affect the life expectancy gap.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"qgjbfNyS76"}],"key":"Qz4jHi82r5"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Model","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"Do6nFeJEA8"}],"identifier":"the-model","label":"The Model","html_id":"the-model","implicit":true,"key":"dIeVf5YrXE"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We built a Bayesian hierarchical panel model that uses 24 years of data from 37 countries.\nIt includes gender gaps in 13 cause-specific death rates from the ","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"WHYKQe8n8S"},{"type":"link","url":"https://www.healthdata.org/research-analysis/gbd","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Burden of Disease (GBD)","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"QpIYyshrqA"}],"urlSource":"https://www.healthdata.org/research-analysis/gbd","key":"DGr5HdAyKB"},{"type":"text","value":" study: alcohol, suicide, homicide, road traffic injuries, cardiovascular disease, diabetes, cancer (neoplasms), lung disease, liver disease, unintentional injury, drug disorders, childhood mortality (under-5), and COVID-19.","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"GxI9LlFxO9"}],"key":"jQ9oQDb0X9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For each cause of death, the model estimates a coefficient (β) that shows how much the life expectancy gap changes for each standard-deviation increase in cause-specific gap.\nPositive coefficients mean that when a death rate gap is larger (men die at higher rates than women), the life expectancy gap is larger (women live longer).","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"Bjk4RRJYVI"}],"key":"HwB9vuOIfl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following figure shows the estimated coefficients; the error bars show 94% credible intervals.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"sFqmr64EvD"}],"key":"FRxaH3IQhU"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/LifeExpectancy//build/blog2_coefficients_l-e58b08abbcb363959aa714a8d501ecb4.png","alt":"Cause of death coefficients for life expectancy gender gap (2000–2023).\nSource: IHME cause-specific death rates, OWID Life Expectancy, analysis by Allen Downey.","width":"80%","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"l5ZDTCheux","urlSource":"figs/blog2_coefficients_le.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cause of death coefficients for life expectancy gender gap (2000–2023).\nSource: IHME cause-specific death rates, OWID Life Expectancy, analysis by Allen Downey.","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"nYE1QC6BMP"}],"key":"msRCJCwQTk"}],"key":"DDB1XpxUiS"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"Xom80bL5GR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Road traffic deaths have the largest coefficient, about 0.6 years per standard deviation, which means that if a country is average in every way except that its gender gap in traffic deaths is one standard deviation above the mean, we expect its life expectancy gap to be 0.6 years above average.\nOther factors with large coefficients are homicide, suicide, cancer, lung disease, and liver disease.","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"gQRCtYtOLq"}],"key":"sV6Ti14nbo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The magnitudes of these coefficients depend on both death rates and the ages of the people affected.\nCancer and respiratory disease have high rates, but they primarily affect older people, so their effect on life expectancy is attenuated.\nHomicide and suicide have lower rates, but they affect younger people, so their effect on life expectancy is amplified.","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"EEAtO55B2h"}],"key":"CwSjxopeWa"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Child mortality affects the youngest people, so each death removes many potential years of life.\nBut the estimated coefficient is small, and the lower bound of the credible interval is close to zero.\nIn most OECD countries the gender gap in child mortality is already small and changes only slowly over time.\nSo the model detects only a weak relationship between variation in child mortality and life expectancy.","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"sbElBU5VQG"}],"key":"HfTgdzBXna"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The coefficient of alcohol is relatively small, but that is partly the result of classification.\nIn the GBD database, alcohol-related deaths are defined narrowly, not including indirect effects — such as liver disease, cancer, road traffic, homicide, suicide, and accidental injury — where alcohol is often a contributing factor.","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"YmNsDZJwwC"}],"key":"ZeVK7aDv5O"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Similarly, we note the underlying effect of smoking, which contributes to gaps in death rates due to cancer, cardiovascular disease, respiratory disease, and possibly COVID-19.","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"hwHdDUds6b"}],"key":"QxJmkjcb4j"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Competing Risks","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"BCVwTYM1rD"}],"identifier":"competing-risks","label":"Competing Risks","html_id":"competing-risks","implicit":true,"key":"MQTs95AzUK"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The coefficients for cardiovascular disease and diabetes are negative, which means that if a country has a larger death rate gap, the model predicts a ","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"txntfIiK0e"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"smaller","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"tSPsxOL7zZ"}],"key":"YaeYNKmjpe"},{"type":"text","value":" life expectancy gap.\nThis is counterintuitive — for example, if more men die of cardiovascular disease, their age-specific death rates should increase and their life expectancy should decrease.\nSo why are these two coefficients negative?","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"vY5vzPwrkQ"}],"key":"P7GmZq1WeY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can get a hint from the following figure, which shows the gap in death rates due to cardiovascular disease.","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"WzECPBOMBb"}],"key":"QLgoCtldvP"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/LifeExpectancy//build/cardiovascular_gap_t-94d0dfd4616cdd9084f6c8bd29866f16.png","alt":"Cardiovascular disease, death rate gender gap (2000–2023), OECD countries.\nSource: Global Burden of Disease from IHME.","width":"80%","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"pipLHblKOf","urlSource":"figs/cardiovascular_gap_timeseries_selected.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cardiovascular disease, death rate gender gap (2000–2023), OECD countries.\nSource: Global Burden of Disease from IHME.","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"pEsPuhvSpK"}],"key":"NdmmocKKb8"}],"key":"FZqvaTKnWB"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"CgTKp6vJfA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In many countries, the gap is negative, meaning that death rates from cardiovascular disease are higher for women.","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"ODS4LaBugz"}],"key":"BzrcOJNKzn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":66,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Furthermore, the gap is positive (higher rates for men) in countries with the best overall health outcomes, like Iceland, and negative (higher rates for women) in countries with the worst outcomes, like Latvia.\nThis is the opposite of the pattern we see with other causes of death, where good overall health is associated with lower death rates, which is associated with smaller gender gaps.","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"key":"xPaD8CPTUm"}],"key":"OiQnUs8gCV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":69,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A possible explanation is ","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"cnbbOEw7C2"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"competing risks","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"Et9lJMKymA"}],"key":"fCO0sP4sEF"},{"type":"text","value":" — where death rates from other causes are low, cardiovascular disease and diabetes become more common causes of death, because they are diseases of aging.\nIf you live long enough to die of cardiovascular disease, it means you avoided the causes of death that affect younger people.","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"Q70HNwdaNJ"}],"key":"DSCANqc9zw"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the regression model, these variables might act as a proxy for overall health outcomes.\nIf a large (positive) death rate gap indicates good outcomes, and good outcomes are associated with smaller life expectancy gaps, that would explain the negative coefficients.","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"key":"NnpbBVVoOl"}],"key":"DVoBSiwyIA"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":74,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Importance","position":{"start":{"line":74,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"key":"KIVX0xdmQ5"}],"identifier":"importance","label":"Importance","html_id":"importance","implicit":true,"key":"uOPmbHuJ4L"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The magnitudes of the coefficients indicate the strength of the statistical relationship between death rate gaps and life expectancy.\nBut they don’t tell us which factors contribute most to differences between countries and changes over time.\nFor that, we’ll use ","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"key":"KjCU1vueN7"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"importance","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"key":"FtboR1SBIt"}],"key":"UHiCFatOXD"},{"type":"text","value":", which is the product of the coefficient and the standard deviation for each cause.","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"key":"sdjRBirPCY"}],"key":"RcgMLDs6ek"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1},"end":{"line":82,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can think of the coefficient as the sensitivity of life expectancy to a hypothetical difference in death rates, and standard deviation as the size of the differences we actually see.\nIf they are both large, the product is large.\nIf either one is small, the product tends to be small.","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"key":"YvTr3lvArQ"}],"key":"z01irpEabz"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following table shows coefficients (years per SD), standard deviations, and importance for each cause.","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"key":"Nn37wN58LO"}],"key":"h538IentwZ"},{"type":"include","file":"../notebooks/tables/importance_measures_le_ihme_nomid_nogrw_y2023_covid.html","literal":false,"lang":"html","children":[{"type":"table","identifier":"t_59f24","label":"T_59f24","children":[{"type":"tableRow","children":[{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"identifier":"t_59f24_level0_col0","label":"T_59f24_level0_col0","class":"col_heading level0 col0","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cause","key":"UVNmUvWeHW"}],"html_id":"t-59f24-level0-col0","key":"ktvOoCrTgq"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"identifier":"t_59f24_level0_col1","label":"T_59f24_level0_col1","class":"col_heading level0 col1","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Standard deviation","key":"NfN8SKBziz"}],"html_id":"t-59f24-level0-col1","key":"o9PpP0GkIw"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"identifier":"t_59f24_level0_col2","label":"T_59f24_level0_col2","class":"col_heading level0 col2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coefficient","key":"wfML3ivWgp"}],"html_id":"t-59f24-level0-col2","key":"ZkwL669sir"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"identifier":"t_59f24_level0_col3","label":"T_59f24_level0_col3","class":"col_heading level0 col3","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Importance","key":"GcxIVKhP7J"}],"html_id":"t-59f24-level0-col3","key":"ASJMrCBiIt"}],"key":"kM7meq5